  "headline": "Per-dataset DP MST selection (eps/3 each for label tables, selection and pair tables; 12 cells paired with E4b, 12/12 verified): private selection picks edges barely more correlated than random pairs (-0.001 to +0.051 |corr|; 0.21 vs oracle 0.29 at n=128, eps=16). Relative to E4b's release, naive over-confidence is lower in only 2/12 cells for the DP tree, 3/12 for the star and 3/12 even for a non-private oracle tree; at n=128, eps=16 the oracle tree lowers it by 0.017-0.019. The DP tree costs naive use 0.004-0.086 accuracy. The noise-aware PFN on the DP-MST release stays calibrated (-0.016 to +0.015) and beats naive in NLL in 12/12 cells, but is worse than E4b's noise-aware PFN on the simpler release in 12/12 cells (by 0.002-0.069 nats).",
  "h4_note_suffix": " E4c-prime selects a per-dataset tree privately, as MST does: at n<=128, eps<=16 the selection is close to random, and even a non-private oracle tree lowers naive over-confidence only at n=128 with eps=4-16 (3/12 cells), because splitting the budget adds more noise than the tree removes. The noise-aware PFN stays calibrated on the DP-MST release and beats naive in NLL in 12/12 cells, though that release is less informative than E4b's.",
